---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'QuiiCast',
  url: 'https://quiicast.com',
  applicationCategory: 'CommunicationApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any',
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    price: '0',
    priceCurrency: 'USD'
  },
  description:
    'QuiiCast is a free screen share app for quick casting your screen to another device — no account, no download, just a 4-digit code.'
};
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Free Screen Share App | QuiiCast — No Sign-Up"
	description="QuiiCast is a free screen share app for quick casting your screen — works on iPhone, Android, and TV. No sign-up, no download, just a 4-digit code."
	path="/"
	jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-slate-200 bg-white px-4 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-brand-600 dark:border-white/10 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-brand-300">
			<span class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
			No sign-up · No download · 100% free
		</span>

		<h1 class="mt-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-900 via-slate-900 to-slate-500 bg-clip-text text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-transparent sm:text-6xl dark:from-white dark:via-white dark:to-slate-400">
			The Fastest Way<br class="hidden sm:block" /> to Screen Share Online
		</h1>

		<p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			QuiiCast lets you cast your screen to any device straight from the browser. Start sharing
			and get a 4-digit code, or enter one to instantly view someone else's screen.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-12 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
		<a class="glass-card group relative overflow-hidden p-7 transition-transform duration-300 hover:-translate-y-1" href="/caster">
			<div class="absolute -top-10 -right-10 h-32 w-32 rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 blur-2xl transition-opacity duration-300 group-hover:opacity-100 opacity-0"></div>
			<span class="flex h-11 w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-2xl bg-brand-500/15 text-brand-300">
				<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" class="h-5 w-5"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="14" rx="2"/><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4"/></svg>
			</span>
			<h2 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Cast your screen</h2>
			<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Share your screen and get a 4-digit code to give to a receiver.</p>
			<span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-brand-300 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1">
				Start casting →
			</span>
		</a>
		<a class="glass-card group relative overflow-hidden p-7 transition-transform duration-300 hover:-translate-y-1" href="/receiver">
			<div class="absolute -top-10 -right-10 h-32 w-32 rounded-full bg-fuchsia-500/20 blur-2xl transition-opacity duration-300 group-hover:opacity-100 opacity-0"></div>
			<span class="flex h-11 w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-2xl bg-fuchsia-500/15 text-fuchsia-300">
				<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" class="h-5 w-5"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg>
			</span>
			<h2 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Receive a screen share</h2>
			<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Enter a 4-digit code to instantly view someone else's screen.</p>
			<span class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-fuchsia-300 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1">
				Enter a code →
			</span>
		</a>
	</div>

	<p class="mt-12 text-center text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-500">
		Works entirely in the browser over a direct peer-to-peer connection. Prefer a local WiFi
		connection for lower latency, or use the default internet mode to connect from anywhere.
		Read more about <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-500 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200">how QuiiCast works</a>,
		browse the <a href="/blog" class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-500 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200">blog</a> for ideas on how to use it,
		or check the <a href="/faq" class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-500 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200">FAQ</a> for quick answers.
	</p>

	<section class="prose mx-auto mt-20 max-w-2xl">
		<h2>What is QuiiCast?</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast is a free screen share app that runs entirely in your browser. There's nothing to
			install and no account to create — open the site, click start, and get a 4-digit code you
			can hand to anyone you want to share your screen with. It's built for the moment you need
			someone to see your screen right now, not fifteen minutes from now after a download
			finishes and an account gets created.
		</p>
		<p>
			Most screen sharing features are bolted onto a bigger video-conferencing platform, alongside
			calendars, waiting rooms, and camera prompts you don't need for a quick screen share.
			QuiiCast does one thing: it lets you quick cast your screen to another device, in real time,
			over a direct peer-to-peer connection, with nothing in between except a short code.
		</p>

		<h2>A quick share app built for speed</h2>
		<p>
			The idea behind QuiiCast is that screen sharing shouldn't need a scheduled meeting. Open the
			<a href="/caster">cast page</a>, share your screen, and read the code out loud, drop it in a
			chat, or text it over. Whoever's receiving types it into the <a href="/receiver">receive
			page</a> and is watching within seconds — that's the entire quick cast flow, start to
			finish, and up to five people can join the same code at once.
		</p>
		<p>
			Because there's no sign-up, there's no account for anyone to forget the password to, and no
			app they need to have already installed. If they have a browser, they can watch — and if a
			code isn't used, it expires on its own after a few minutes, so nothing is left running once
			you're done.
		</p>

		<h2>Screen share on iPhone, Android, and any browser</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast works across devices without a native app to install. On Android, you can both cast
			and receive a screen share directly from Chrome or any modern mobile browser. On iPhone,
			Apple's own platform restrictions mean Safari can't capture the screen to cast it out —
			that's a limitation of iOS itself, not QuiiCast — but an iPhone can receive a screen share
			from someone else without any trouble at all. So whether you're on an iPhone watching
			someone else's screen, or on Android doing either side of the connection, QuiiCast works the
			same way it does on desktop: a code, and a connection.
		</p>

		<h2>Cast your screen to a TV</h2>
		<p>
			If your TV has its own browser — most modern smart TVs do — you can screen cast to a TV the
			same way you'd share to any other device: open the receive page on the TV, type in the code
			shown on your laptop, and your screen appears on the bigger display. Turning on "Prefer
			local WiFi" before you start keeps the video on your home network instead of routing it over
			the internet, which usually means less delay when the TV and your laptop are a few feet
			apart. Read more about
			<a href="/blog/casting-to-a-tv-on-same-wifi">casting to a TV on the same WiFi</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Devices like Roku are a different case worth knowing about: Roku's operating system doesn't
			include a general-purpose web browser, so there's no way to open a receive page directly on
			a Roku itself. If you're specifically trying to
			<a href="/blog/how-to-screen-cast-to-roku">screen cast to Roku</a>, a smart TV with its own
			browser — or a laptop connected to the TV by cable — will get you further than trying to
			load a website directly on the Roku.
		</p>

		<h2>A lighter alternative to screen recorders</h2>
		<p>
			Tools like Screencast-O-Matic are built around recording — capturing a video file you can
			edit and publish later. QuiiCast isn't a recorder. There's no video file, no export step,
			and nothing saved anywhere once the session ends. If what you actually need is for someone
			to see your screen right now, a recording tool is more setup than the moment calls for.
			QuiiCast is built specifically for that instant, live case — screen sharing, not screen
			recording.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
